Phase 5 — approvals, durable effect receipts, kernel hardening, attack suite (items 1, 2, 3, 6)
========================================================================
Phase context: Future-tasks Phase 5 ("approvals, protected-effect
receipts and review"); items 4 (tracing/OTel) and 5 (review-surface
depth) and item 6 (docs/52 suite — delivered here) tracked per item
below.
Revision: commit "Phase 5 (items 1,2,3,6) ..." on main; base 7078d77
Environment: macOS darwin 25.5.0 arm64, local dev profile, UTC

WHAT CHANGED
---------------------------------------------------------------------
- crates/effects (canonical owner, docs/81): the protected-effect
  receipt chain PROMOTED from the checkpoint prototype — hash-linked
  receipts binding approval/capability/call/result digests; Chain
  (pure) + Ledger (append-only JSONL, durable across restarts,
  verify-before-extend, on-disk tamper detection). The checkpoint
  prototype stays as-is (its consumers unchanged); the canonical
  production ledger is this crate. crates/effects enters the product
  closure.
- migration v8: durable `approvals` table (SQLite) — pending effect
  approvals survive a Core kill; decisions are first-wins (a replayed
  ApproveEffect after a restart cannot re-resolve).
- DurableApprovalGate + LiveGrants: under execution_mode=approvals
  (Phase 4.2 settings — the mode is a REAL consumer now), a kernel-
  denied Write/External effect PERSISTS a pending approval and the tool
  call BLOCKS on the operator decision; approval appends a LIVE
  provisional grant (decision granted, never the blanket capability);
  the kernel re-check then allows exactly the approved effect. OneAgent
  re-snapshots the grant set on EVERY tool call, so mid-run approvals
  take effect.
- ApproveEffect/DenyEffect surface RPCs (proto 19 → 21): resolve by
  approval_id; first decision wins; replays and unknown ids refused
  with typed errors.
- Kernel hardening (docs/52 § path escape — defense in depth, found by
  the new attack suite): ANY parent-directory component in a
  protected-effect path argument is denied — prefix lists alone missed
  mid-path traversal (foo/../../etc/shadow).
- docs/52 attack suite (always-on tests, policy_attack_suite.rs): path
  escape denied despite write grants; approval decisions bind intent
  HASHES (replay/unknown/expiry refused; mutated arguments change the
  hash and stay unapproved); effect receipts bind digests never raw
  payloads (hostile ledger tamper detected); hostile tool RESULTS
  cannot self-grant (the kernel decides from grants, not model
  content).

E2E PROOFS (approvals_e2e, REAL daemon + REAL model endpoint; 3 tests)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
  approve_unblocks_the_protected_effect ................ ok
  - execution_mode=approvals persisted via settings (Phase 4.2)
  - the model's change.apply attempt PERSISTS a pending approval and
    the tool call blocks (no worktree write while pending)
  - ApproveEffect resolves it; the blocked effect proceeds; the edit
    REALLY lands in the worktree
  - replaying the decision is refused (first decision wins)
  deny_refuses_the_protected_effect_but_completes ...... ok
  - DenyEffect: the worktree stays byte-identical; the task still
    completes (the model is told) — never left hanging
  pending_approval_survives_a_core_kill ................ ok
  - the durable row survives the death of the core that created it

MUTATION CHECK (docs/55), Phase 4.2-adjacent for this phase:
  The approval gate's own control is the mode toggle: in default mode
  the same edit applies with NO approval flow (proven by
  settings_e2e's default-mode control test); in approvals mode the
  identical effect cannot proceed without a decision (proven here).
  Additionally the live-grant mechanism is validated by the E2E's
  replay check (a second approve cannot re-unblock).

IN-PROGRESS DURABILITY NOTE (honest scope)
---------------------------------------------------------------------
The restart-durability E2E iteration revealed that a Core restart
while an approval is pending RESUMES the task (Phase 2.5 boot replay)
which re-requests the approval under a NEW id (the old row stays
pending). The operator flow is to approve the LIVE request (the
desktop Needs-Attention card reads pending_for_task). Automating
stale-row cleanup (superseded state) is recorded as follow-up
hardening; the durable-store semantics (first decision wins) are
already enforced.

